  "query_preview": "..\nRest of Today through Sunday...\n\nStrong upper level low is working through the central portion of\nthe state late this morning. Showers have exited to the east of \nthe forecast area for the most part as subsidence builds in on the\nback side of the system, with strong west/northwest winds expected\nfor the rest of this afternoon into the early evening hours.\nStrongest winds are expected over the western foothills (US-191\ncorridor), and over Custer/Fallon/Carter Counties where a High\nWind Warning is in effect for occasional gusts around 60 mph\ntoday. \n\nWinds will diminish this evening and a mostly clear and much\ncalmer overnight is forecast. Models are hinting at some fog\npotential for the northeastern zones and as far west as Billings\nalong the Yellowstone River valley. Currently have diminishing\nnorthwest winds in the forecast (not very favorable for fog\ndevelopment), but if the winds do turn north/northeast a bit \nfaster than currently advertised fog could be a possibility with a\nsaturated near surface layer forecast. Went ahead and added a\nmention of fog to the forecast, will re-evaluate this evening and\nsee if the wind forecast comes into a more favorable direction\ntonight. Lows will be cool in the 30s west and low to mid 40s\neast. Forecast temperatures are in the Frost range for the western\nfoothills and valleys, so protect any vegitation that might get\ndamaged. \n\nMostly sunny and dry day on Tuesday with breezy easterly winds\nhelping to hold temperatures in the 65-75 degree range. \n\nAn upper level trof approaches the area from the northwest on \nWednesday, but the energy with it splits in two with a good chunk \ndropping south along the west coast on Wednesday, while most of \nthe rest pushes across southern Canada. This splitting system \nwon't generate much more than a few more clouds over the area, but\nit will push a reinforcing shot of cooler air into the area on \nnortherly winds, holding temperatures mainly in the 60s, though SE\nareas will see low to mid-70s ahead of the wind shift. \n\nThursday through the weekend...The upper low along the Pacific\nCoast will slowly drift northeastward toward our area over\nThursday and Friday, and then either drift east through Montana\nSaturday or just open up and dissipate over the area.\nSouthwesterly winds aloft will over-run increasing southeasterly\nsurface winds Thursday and Friday. This sets up a decent\nshower/thunderstorm pattern heading into the weekend. Energy and\nPacific moisture ride in on the southwest flow aloft, and plains\nmoisture and warmer temperatures arrive with the low level\nsoutheast flow. NBM is showing isolated to scattered\nshowers/thunderstorms Thursday/Thursday night for most of the\narea, with higher chances over the western mountains. By Friday\nprecipitation probabilities ramp up into the 60 to 80 percent\nrange across the area through Saturday morning. Probabilities for\nhalf an inch or more of precipitation Fri-Sat currently sit at \n30% in Livingston, 50% in Billings, 60% in Miles City, and 70% in \nBaker. As the upper low drifts into the area the dynamics shift  \nto more of an instability shower mode and precipitation chances \ntrend downward into Saturday night with Sunday looking mainly dry\nat this point. Thursday/Friday look like the warmest days of the \nweek with highs in the 70s, maybe some lower 80s along the Wyoming\nborder from Sheridan county eastward. Temperatures stay mainly in\nthe lower 70s Saturday and Sunday. Chambers",
